          ":question/problem": "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+4y+2z &=3 \\\\ y &=-1 \\\\ z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form.\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=5 \\\\ y &=-1 \\\\ z &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we have the solution $x=5$, $y=-1$, and $z=1.$"
